        scenario: `Sofia, an experienced agency therapist, arrives for her first shift at Harewood Bay Hotel and Spa on the Devon coast, a venue whose treatments she has never delivered and whose room layout she has never seen. Her first booking is a 90-minute full-body massage for a regular guest, Mrs Hale, who is known to the resident team as observant and exacting. Sofia has twenty minutes before the appointment. Rather than reviewing her strokes, she spends the time in the treatment room: repositioning the oil pumps within one-handed reach of the couch, checking the towel sizes and practising the house turnover with the face cradle twice, and walking the path she will take around the couch.`,
        insight: `Sofia understands that on an unfamiliar site the threat to quality is not technique, which travels with her, but continuity, which depends on the room. By rehearsing the logistics in advance she ensures her contact never breaks to hunt for oil, her draping is sized to the house towels, and her turnover is silent on the first attempt. Mrs Hale later tells reception the new therapist never seemed to leave her for a moment. The professional lesson: flow is prepared, not improvised, and agency therapists who invest their setup time in the room, not the routine, are the ones venues request again.`,
